5.1.0
2026-06-04
track-changes v5.1 — `polish` broadened from dictation cleanup to a FULL editorial pass.
  polish now does the work a copy-editor does on newly dictated prose — split run-on
  sentences, reorder clauses for flow, tighten wordiness, smooth awkward phrasing — in
  addition to the recognition/grammar/dropped-word fixes. It stays meaning-preserving and
  diff-scoped (it only ever touches prose newly dictated since the git baseline, never the
  vetted/committed corpus), and every change is still an ordinary track-changes `<mark>`
  reviewed via `/tc accept|reject`. Bright line #2 is split: the hard line is MEANING — never
  drop or weaken a qualifier, hedge, scope phrase, or modal (a qualifier-preservation rule for
  restructures), never alter a fact/number; and an OBVIOUS error (a broken/nonsensical sentence,
  e.g. a stray question in declarative prose) is now MARKED with a best-guess fix, not demoted
  to a prose-only suggestion. Restructure mark-UX: a restructure wraps the WHOLE sentence as one
  replacement mark (not a scatter of char-diffs); the run reports four buckets (corrections /
  restructures / left+flagged / suggested); sentence relocations across paragraphs are
  suggested, not applied. Engine fix: smart-quote contractions/possessives (it's, they're,
  item's) are no longer mis-flagged as protected tokens — typographic apostrophes/quotes/dashes
  are folded to ASCII before the non-ASCII protected-token test, so genuine symbols/Greek (αvhq)
  and the prime ′ stay protected while ordinary contractions remain polishable. No hook or
  settings change; the mark grammar, the hooks, and track-changes + verified-import are
  UNCHANGED. Backward-compatible — no document migration.

5.0.0
2026-06-03
track-changes v5 — `polish` joins as the THIRD cooperating skill; the suite is now
  track-changes + verified-import + polish, sharing one tc_core package.
5.0.0 major: `polish` (`/polish [file]`) cleans up VOICE-DICTATED prose — speech-recognition
  errors, grammar, dropped words — in an existing tracked .md/.qmd file, surfacing every fix
  as an ordinary track-changes `<mark>` (reviewable via `/tc accept|reject`). It is opt-in and
  default-OFF: the explicit `/polish` invocation IS the opt-in (no marker file). Bright line —
  it NEVER auto-corrects an unrecognized token (jargon/code/math/domain term): such tokens are
  left + flagged, never silently changed; a meaning-affecting fix is surfaced as a mark, not
  applied silently. polish adds NO new hooks and NO settings change — its prose fixes flow
  through the EXISTING track-changes PreToolUse hook unchanged; on a track-changes-tracked file
  fixes become marks, on an untracked file it offers a one-time direct polish (no marks) or to
  enable tracking first. polish_engine.py reuses track-changes' own `tc_core.activation` for the
  authoritative tracked-check and imports tc_core from track-changes/lib (no duplicate copy —
  same shared-core pattern as verified-import). Files: polish/SKILL.md, polish/lib/polish-cli.sh,
  polish/lib/polish_engine.py → ~/.claude/skills/polish/. No view-time "dictated lens" (an earlier
  build's render-time second color was retired after a source-vs-executed token-stream drift; `git
  diff` is the authoritative "what did I change" view). Existing tracked documents need NO
  conversion; the mark grammar, hooks, and two prior skills are UNCHANGED.
- The hook count stays SIX (five track-changes + one verified-import); polish owns no hook.
  settings-patch.json is byte-unchanged from v4. The bootstrap version check is corrected to
  5.0.0 (the published 4.x bootstrap still referenced 4.0.0 — see the 4.1.0 note below).

4.1.0
2026-05-31
track-changes v4.1 — per-file activation key renamed `track-changes` -> `tc-track`.
  Rolled into the source-of-record HERE for the first time: 4.1.0 was published to the Pages
  site and installed locally on 2026-05-31 but was never committed back to this source repo, so
  the v4/ snapshot stayed at 4.0.0. v5 reconciles the source up to the published 4.1.0 content
  (activation.py, tc-cli.sh, tc-common.sh, tc-mark.sh, SKILL.md, digest.md, latex.md) before
  adding polish. The old `track-changes:` YAML key is a RESERVED Quarto field (Quarto accepts only
  accept/reject/all and errors on true/false), so it broke every .qmd/.md render; `tc-track` is an
  unknown key Quarto passes through untouched. BREAKING for files using the old key — swap them to
  `tc-track`. Skill name, the `/tc` command, the mark grammar, the hooks, and `.tc-tracked` folder
  markers are unchanged. Also hardened `/draft` so a Skill-tool invocation (which does not run the
  `!bash` sentinel writer) self-verifies and falls back to running draft-on.sh via Bash.

4.0.0
2026-05-26
track-changes v4 — import-verification paradigm change (LLM-judgment).
4.0.0 major: the `verified-import` mechanical content gate is REMOVED. v3 verified an
  AI-converted block against the named source by a content-word multiset compare and
  blocked fail-closed on any discrepancy — too strict in practice, repeatedly tripping
  faithful math/code imports (the EOQ false-reject). v4 trusts the LLM to import
  faithfully: a live pending-import makes the `verified-import` PreToolUse hook write the
  sha-bound exemption sentinel UNCONDITIONALLY (no comparison), so the converted block
  lands CLEAN by default via the exemption the always-on `track-changes` skill honors.
  The model SELF-MARKS only genuinely significant changes (altered meaning — added/removed
  sentence or clause, changed quantity/term/formula) in track-changes marks; minor diffs
  (reflow, reformatting, equivalent notation) land clean with no mark. "Verified" is
  redefined from "mechanically content-word-equal" to "LLM-asserted faithful, with
  significant changes marked for review." The dead gate code
  (`normalized_equal`/`strip_markup`/`_content_words`) is deleted; the pending record is
  slimmed to `{target, source_path, range, expires}`; the pending TTL default is 120→300 s
  (user-overridable); the `/import` CLI now strips a leading `@` file-reference prefix from
  the source and target args. No change to the track-changes mark grammar, the F2
  single-group PreToolUse hook order (verified-import before track-changes), or the
  two-skill install. Existing tracked documents need NO conversion. R-category tests
  rewritten for the LLM-judgment model (the v3 gate-behavior fixtures retired).
The single v2 skill is decomposed into two: a narrowed always-on `track-changes`
  mark-gate and a NEW opt-in `verified-import` skill (`/import`) for inserting a
  vetted source into a tracked document. The skills share one tc_core package
  (track-changes/lib/tc_core — not duplicated); verified-import imports it.
- SessionStart now injects a compact reference/digest.md (~2.9 KB) instead of the
  full SKILL.md — ~93.5% smaller, well under the 40 KB inline cap; full SKILL.md is
  lazy-loaded on demand via "see SKILL.md §N" pointers.
- Always-on surface narrowed (idea.md scope-decomposition thrust): §0 import wrappers
  moved out to verified-import; §5 cross-file mark renumbering removed; §6
  edit-inside-non-rendering-construct sibling removed (such edits now route to /draft).
  Brand-new-block sibling (mechanism-1, md/qmd) is kept; a brand-new LaTeX \section/env
  routes to /draft.
- Persistent daemon DROPPED after measurement (in-process pre_tool_use p50 ~41.5 ms,
  ~6x under the keep threshold; the daemon's ~13.5 ms/edit saving did not justify a
  294-line localhost-TCP subsystem). In-process is now the sole hook path.
- /import stages a target-keyed pending-import; on a live pending-import the
  verified-import hook writes a one-shot exemption sentinel that the track-changes gate
  consumes — a verified import lands clean (no marks). The v3 content gate is removed
  (see the 4.0.0 entry above).
- Clean break (Q2): the v3 mark grammar is UNCHANGED — existing tracked documents need
  NO conversion; upgrading only re-installs files and registers the two-skill hook set
  (verified-import and track-changes stacked in one PreToolUse matcher group). /tc migrate
  (v1->v2 marks) is retained for legacy v1 marks.
